WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:03.336 line:0%
-What defines a home, really?

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:06.239 line:0%
Is it just
four walls and a roof?

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.074 line:0%
Or is it
something more than that?

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:13.446
I can tell you that, for me,
home is where my wine fridge is.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.580
[ Spectators mumbling ]

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.817
Now, I've personally
never been unhoused,

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:21.554
but there was one summer where
they were doing construction

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:25.325
on our New York home,
and I had to stay at The Plaza.

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.860
At the end of those three weeks,
I felt like I was getting out

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.729
of Shawshank.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.097
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.965
We're here with Maya Rudolph.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.965 --> 00:00:37.169
That was a clip
from her new series, "Loot."

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:40.840
So, you are married
to a billionaire, Adam Scott.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.508
-Yes, Adam Scott was my husband,

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.979
who's a very powerful
businessman,

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:47.613
one of the richest men
in the world,

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:50.350
and then we get divorced
and I end up with $87 billion.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.685
This is not real life, guys.
This is the show.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.286
Don't get confused.
-Yeah.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:57.256
And then, you know,
she's been with him

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:58.591
her whole life,
like since their 20s,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.859
helped him build the company.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.195
So now she's trying to find
meaning in her life

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:04.897
with $87 billion.

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:07.066
-There --
You shoot in a mansion.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:10.103
-Oh, yeah.
We shot at "The One."

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.172
Have you ever been in a house
that has a name?

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.274
-No.
-This house was called The One.

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:15.574
-Wow.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:19.878
-And it was -- I think it was
listed for $500 million.

00:01:19.878 --> 00:01:23.315
And it was pretty insane.

00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:25.785
-Yeah. For $500 million,
it would have been really funny

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.988
if you were like,
"And it's like, 'Eh.'"

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.758
-"I mean, I've seen better."

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:36.863
It was -- Like, the first day,
we shot at the indoor pool,

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.098
and I was like,
"This is pretty cool.

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:39.932
There's an indoor pool
in here, huh?"

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:41.834
-[ Laughs ]
And then --

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.336
I feel like
I just heard Shoemaker laugh.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:50.510
And then I went outside,
and there were four other pools.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.645
And then I went upstairs
to the master bedroom,

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:55.214
and there is a pool off the
balcony of the master bedroom,

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:56.482
but it's see-through.

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:59.652
So if, like, you want to watch
your lover swim --

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.788
Like, "Do the backstroke,
sweetheart.

00:02:02.788 --> 00:02:04.224
Do me a favor."

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:07.226
Get me a cup of coffee and do
the backstroke, sweetheart.

00:02:07.226 --> 00:02:10.162
Then do the breaststroke,
sweetheart.

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:14.433
Alright, now
just do the dog thing.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.435
Paddle like a dog."

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.838
-The dog paddle.
-What is it? Oh, dog paddle.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.639
"The dog thing."
-You had all the parts.

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:22.508
-I know. I gave up.
-"Do the dog thing.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:24.476
Paddle like a dog.
What's that called?"

00:02:24.476 --> 00:02:26.312
Dog paddle.
-It's called the dumbwaiter.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.914
It's called the dumbwaiter.

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:30.750
-You also got to work with
animals in this, some alpacas?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.185
-I did.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.021
It was not my first Alpaca,
which know is a big surprise,

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.956
because when
you're in comedy, apparently,

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:39.259
you work with
a lot of strange animals.

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:40.793
-Yeah.
-Guess who I met this morning.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.895
-Who'd you meet this morning?

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:46.565
-Trumpet, the Westminster
Dog Kennel champion, the winner.

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:48.434
-The bloodhound, right?
-Yeah.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:49.702
-Yeah.
-I have news for you.

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.038
He's hot.
-Oh!

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.673
-I have a little crush on him.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.341
I went to pet him.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.009
He's so lovely.
-Okay.

00:02:57.009 --> 00:02:58.110
He's so sweet.
He smelled so good.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.879
He's 4 years old.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.849
-And then he gave me his paw.
His paw was like this big.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.585
But then he kind of
just kept climbing me.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:08.120
-Okay.
-And I'm in love.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.923
-Where -- I obviously
have a bunch of follow-ups,

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:14.294
beginning with, where do you
meet a dog like this?

00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:17.163
-At the "Today" show, Seth.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.530
On Grindr, Seth.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.832
On Grindr.

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:22.635
-You have -- Yeah,
what's the new Grindr for dogs?

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.670
Oh, Dog Grindr.
-It's called Dog Paddler.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.339
-Dog Paddler!

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:30.076
-If you look on Dog Paddler,
you can find --

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.545
-And you either
paddle left or paddle right.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.713
-If you paddle right, you get

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:36.382
the champion of the
Westminster Dog Show.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.817
He was
so beautiful and so gorgeous.

00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:41.120
But he's --
A bloodhound, apparently,

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.189
is the only noi-- noise? --

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.723
Noise? Let me try that again.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:47.693
-A bloodhound is the only
nose that can stand up in court.

00:03:47.693 --> 00:03:50.163
No other nose is allowed
to be listened to.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:51.564
-Oh, wow!

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:55.735
So, legally, a bloodhound can go
in and point at a murderer.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:59.171
-And say, "That's the guy
who murdered my wife."

00:03:59.171 --> 00:04:00.706
-[ Barks ]

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:04.177
Do your best bloodhound,
how that would sound, if that --

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:06.813
[ Barking ] Ohhhh!

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:13.385
[ Barking ] Ohhhhhhhhh!

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:14.587
-So, this is interesting.

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:16.255
When you said
they're the only nose,

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.091
I assume that, like,
experts would bring them in

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:19.858
for other cases,
but it's only in a case

00:04:19.858 --> 00:04:21.861
where their wife or husband
has been murdered.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.664
-Where the the bloodhound's wife
was murdered.

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:28.501
-So they're only allowed to
testify if it's kin.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.602
-Yeah. Like...

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:32.304
[ Barking ] "That's my cousin!

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:34.474
You killed my cousin!"

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.509
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:41.280
-You showed up at "SNL,"
the season finale,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.415
for our friend Natasha Lyonne.

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:47.387
And that was such a joy for me,
because she --

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:49.789
I feel like she embodies,
like, New York

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.156
and what's cool about New York
in the same way that "SNL" does.

00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:52.358
-Agreed.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:54.994
-And it just --
When did you first meet Natasha?

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:58.898
-I met her, actually, when
I first moved here to do "SNL."

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.434
She was one of my first
non-"SNL" friends.

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:03.802
And I think I was with Jimmy.

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:05.204
We were at the Mercer bar,

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:07.040
and this little person came up
to me and was like,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.708
"Hey, you want to be in
a fashion show?"

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:11.543
And it was Natasha.

00:05:11.543 --> 00:05:14.947
And her friend Chloe Sevigny
and Tara Subkoff

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.350
were doing their line,
Imitation of Christ,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.518
and they asked if we'd be in it.

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:20.619
And I was really excited,
because I was like,

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:22.254
"You want me to be
in a fashion show?"

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:23.756
Funny thing is, they wanted me

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:26.926
to be the emcee
in the fashion show.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:28.094
'Cause when you're in comedy,

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:30.763
you don't model
in the fashion show, Seth.

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.733
You're the emcee.
But it was so cool and fun.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.368
But, like,
she's one of those people

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.236
that when you meet your people,
you find them right away.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:38.504
We were instantly friends.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:42.041
And, you know,
she spent so much time here,

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.743
backstage with us, hanging out,
and she's a part of those

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:45.978
early years for us.

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:49.582
So it was really meaningful
for her to be here

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:51.917
in what she called, like --
in her friends' house.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.285
She had so much respect for it.

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:55.087
And she's so funny
and such a joy.

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.922
-It's also that thing --
'Cause it doesn't happen

00:05:56.922 --> 00:05:59.224
all the time, because I think,
you know, understandably,

00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:01.294
people have a lot of nerves,
and over the course of the show,

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.395
they find their way to joy.

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:03.496
-Yeah.

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:04.997
-She walked out
to do that monologue,

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:07.666
and it was just, like,
full joy, full excitement.

00:06:07.666 --> 00:06:09.067
-Happy as a clam.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:10.169
-Yeah. And it was great to
see you there,

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:11.236
as well, and Fred.

00:06:11.236 --> 00:06:12.405
-It was so nice, yeah.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.939
-And it's always nice
to have you here.

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.107
Thank you so much, Maya.
-Oh, my God.

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:19.745
Next time I come, we got to sing
"Gays in Space,"

00:06:19.745 --> 00:06:21.147
'cause it's Pride Week.

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:22.948
-Yeah, it's Pride Week.
-Pride Week.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:24.449
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:26.786
-You can do -- If you want
to do a little now.

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:29.321
-I mean, if you want me to.

00:06:29.321 --> 00:06:31.990
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.729
What if I was like,
"'Gays in Space' in G, fellas."

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.865
Nobody knows it.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:39.899
But I promise
I'll do it next time.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.033
-Okay, great.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:42.501
You guys, that's Maya Rudolph,
everybody!

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:45.404
"Loot" premieres on
Apple TV+ tomorrow.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.073
We'll be right back with
Werner Herzog.

